  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nMid-level high pressure continues to dominate the weather pattern \nthroughout the forecast period. The tropical moisture riding around \nthe southern periphery of this high pressure system that provided \nthe Coastal Bend with much needed rain overnight will continue to \npush inland with the overall moisture thinning out throughout the \nday. CAMs such as the 12Z HRRR show that today's rain activity along \nthe coast will be confined to this morning, with further convection \ndeveloping inland (west of Hwy 77) in the early afternoon. \nAdditional accumulations of 1-2 inches in new storms (any rain is \nwelcomed to help combat the drought setting in). \n\nAfter today, we slip more into this high pressure influence. So long \nwill be the days of 2.00\"+ PWATs as mid-levels dry out leaving most \nof the area with PWATs between 1.3-1.7\" (at or slightly below the \n50th percentile climatologically speaking). This translates to more \nisolated seabreeze activity through the upcoming week, but most \nplaces have a better chance of staying dry. At the surface, we \nswitch from an easterly surface wind fetch to the normal \nsoutheasterly flow by tonight. Tonight's high tide cycle will likely \nbe the greatest threat for coastal flooding before the wave energy \nsubsides through the late weekend and into the upcoming week. \n\nThe above average temperatures continue to be the biggest hazard in \nthe coming week with heat indices ranging from 105-110F and the \nHeatRisk climbing to Major/Extreme (level 3 and 4 of 4) by the \nupcoming mid-week time period. Continue to practice heat safety and \nhydrate, hydrate, hydrate!",
